the coronavirus is taking a  serious toll on doctors and nurses  mental health as america's death toll  approaches 200 thousand  the largest study of front-line medical  workers at the height of the pandemic in  new york city found  57 percent of them had acute stress  48 percent saw depressive symptoms and  nearly 75 percent  experienced insomnia senior medical  correspondent dr taran narula spoke with  doctors and nurses in new york city  also new mexico and florida about the  stress they face  you train expecting that someday there  may be a catastrophe but living it  is something i don't think you ever  fully are  prepared for we may walk into the  building  take care of our patients do what we  have to do but on the inside  you know we're suffering we just went  from  one patient to another without having  any time  and between to process what just  happened it's made me almost rethink  my career choice rethink what it is to  be a nurse  describe for me the toll that this is  taken on you  emotionally and mentally i don't  honestly know if i've been this tired in  my life  we're all tired emotionally  intellectually  spiritually i meet criteria from mild  depression  there are emotional places in my icu i  don't like going past  because of cases i remember i'm a 24  year old with children who died from a  stroke  a 25 year old man who died with his  parent and his wife at his bedside  still hurt i have like  terrible insomnia i experience  nightmares  i close my eyes and i see the patient  that i took care of last  i see the images of suffering and i  think that's going to scar me for life  nightmares about like my previous  assignment like did i do everything that  i need to do  i feel like the anxiety is kind of just  following me into sleep and so i'm not  getting adequate rest you tried to fall  asleep but my mind was  completely replaying what i went through  throughout the day would i really have  to decide  who lives and who dies i think  surprisingly  we found that about over 50 percent of  individuals  had acute stress disorder which may  develop actually in post-traumatic  stress disorder we also found that  nearly half of our participants  developed depressive symptoms i'm not  going to leave tomorrow will i bring  this home to my family  what am i going to do just questions  questions  questions having to kind of  absorb what family members would usually  absorb where they're at the end of life  and so now we're we're absorbing that  grief in those moments and maybe  multiple times in a shift  so that's difficult do you worry about  ptsd  a lot for the most part i may be  exhibiting  slight symptoms of ptsd i can feel a  difference in my personality  because i'm usually a bubbly person and  i think i've lost that  my fuse is shorter i'm more irritable  i think i'm just afraid it's like being  a rope where you just twist and you  twist and eventually  you fray why do you think it's so  difficult for  health care workers and medical  professionals to talk about  issues around mental health like  depression anxiety  fear we're kind of expected to just  suck it up to compartmentalize  everything we see  we feel as though we won't be valued in  our profession if we  speak on what we live with on our daily  lives and what's kind of part of us  the white coat makes a great straight  jacket that we don't  talk about the things we need to and  that needs to stop  right now in coba the idea of being the  healthcare hero  really adds this increased burden on  individuals  when you see these billboards of  healthcare heroes and our heroes wear  scrubs and so on and so forth  and you look at yourself and you realize  i'm no hero  i'm broken i'm hurting it's just another  point of  self-critique that i am supposed to be  this hero now  and i'm not and it's another point of  pain  we're seen as resilient that we're  unbreakable  and if we don't address what we have  going on internally it's really  difficult to adequately care for  our patients covet 19 has provided us an  ability to talk about our own  vulnerabilities and to say  that's okay because from vulnerability  actually that's the birthplace for  courage  it's a birthplace of joy and i think  it's really really important  to be okay with that  and our doctor tara narula joins us now  dr nerol that is a  very powerful story you could hear it in  those people's voices what they were  going through uh the complicated  reaction to being called a hero  all the stress that they bear what can  we as patients as the public do  to help uh workers who are struggling  with their mental health  yeah i know tony i'm emotional just  watching it back but you know i think  the first step is really  normalizing this and that's what  everybody said we need to begin to talk  about this  openly family and friends and co-workers  should know they can ask  their friends how are you doing how are  you feeling and not just  ask but really listen institutions and  hospital systems need to build in  support and also  create a culture where it's okay to talk  openly where you don't feel you'll be  looked down upon  but one of the ironies of this is that  it's really our emotions  our vulnerability as healthcare  providers that make us the best healers  and  dr abdallah said very very well when you  start to lose that  we start to lose the ability to connect  to someone who's suffering that's when  it's time to actually leave  the healthcare profession yeah that's  definitely true very powerful story dr  taran rula thank you very much
 

